r"><name><surname>Sass</surname><given-names>GL</given-names></name><name><surname>Pannuti</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Lucchesi</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Male-specific lethal complex of <italic>Drosophila </italic>targets activated regions of the X chromosome for chromatin remodeling.</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA</source><year>2003</year><volume>100</volume><fpage>8287</fpage><lpage>8291</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12829796</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1073/pnas.1332749100</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mendjan</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Taipale</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Kind</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Holz</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Gebhardt</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Schelder</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Vermeulen</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Buscaino</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Duncan</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Mueller</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal></etal></person-group><article-title>Nuclear pore components are involved in the transcriptional regulation of dosage compensation in <italic>Drosophila</italic>.</article-title><source>Mol Cell</source><year>2006</year><volume>21</volume><fpage>811</fpage><lpage>823</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16543150</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.molcel.2006.02.007</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wutz</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>RNAs templating chromatin structure for dosage compensation in animals.</article-title><source>BioEssays</source><year>2003</year><volume>25</volume><fpage>434</fpage><lpage>442</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12717814</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/bies.10274</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Clustered short DNA motifs recruit the DCC in <italic>C. elegans</italic>. It is proposed that the DCC (shown as the multicolored globular structure above the X chromosome (X)) recognizes and binds at four specific sites (<italic>rex-1</italic>, <italic>rex-2</italic>, <italic>rex-3 </italic>and <italic>rex-4</italic>) and then spreads or diffuses to the rest of the X chromosome. The <italic>rex </italic>sites have high densities of at least two degenerate DNA motifs (motif A, red; motif B, blue) and this clustering is required to bind the DCC. The same motifs occur on autosomes (A), but they are not clustered and thus do not bind the DCC.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gb-2007-8-2-204-1"/></fig></sec></back></article>